// @expect-error Struct 'Chain' contains itself: field 'next' of 'Chain' has type 'Chain?'. A struct keeps its fields inside itself, so every 'Chain' would contain another 'Chain' without end. Make 'Chain' a class, as in 'class Chain { ... }', because a class field only refers to another object.
// @expect-error Struct 'Wheel' contains itself: field 'spoke' of 'Wheel' has type 'Spoke' and field 'hub' of 'Spoke' has type 'Wheel?'. A struct keeps its fields inside itself, so every 'Wheel' would contain another 'Wheel' without end. Make 'Spoke' a class, as in 'class Spoke { ... }', because a class field only refers to another object.
// @expect-error Struct 'Spoke' contains itself: field 'hub' of 'Spoke' has type 'Wheel?' and field 'spoke' of 'Wheel' has type 'Spoke'. A struct keeps its fields inside itself, so every 'Spoke' would contain another 'Spoke' without end. Make 'Wheel' a class, as in 'class Wheel { ... }', because a class field only refers to another object.
// A struct stores its fields inline, so no chain of by-value fields may loop
// back to the struct itself, directly or through another struct.
struct Chain {
    Chain? next;
    long value = 0;
}

struct Wheel {
    Spoke spoke;
}

struct Spoke {
    Wheel? hub;
}

main() -> int {
    return 0;
}
